  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "ba644a9cc7952a17",
      "path": "src/requests/utils.py",
      "line": 222,
      "category": "Correctness",
      "severity": "Medium",
      "body": "When `NETRC` is unset, this loop expands the default `~/.netrc` paths. `expanduser` can raise `KeyError` when no `HOME` is defined and the passwd lookup cannot resolve the current UID (the removed handling explicitly covered this case). That `KeyError` is not covered by the remaining outer `except`, so otherwise ordinary requests now fail during netrc lookup instead of continuing without netrc credentials. Restore the `KeyError` handling around `expanduser` (returning no auth), or handle it equivalently.",
      "snippet": "        for f in netrc_locations:\n            loc = os.path.expanduser(f)\n            if os.path.exists(loc):"
    },
    {
      "id": "92c6db7d1710f772",
      "path": "src/requests/utils.py",
      "line": 222,
      "category": "Tests",
      "severity": "Low",
      "body": "The removal of the local `KeyError` handling changes `get_netrc_auth` from returning no auth to propagating `KeyError` when home-directory expansion fails while checking the default `~/.netrc` locations. The added tests only set `NETRC` to an explicit path, so they never execute this fallback branch. Add a regression test that unsets `NETRC`, mocks `requests.utils.os.path.expanduser` to raise `KeyError`, and asserts the intended new result (currently, that `get_netrc_auth(...)` raises `KeyError`; if graceful no-auth behavior remains intended, assert `None` and restore handling). This makes the compatibility/error-path decision explicit.",
      "snippet": "            loc = os.path.expanduser(f)"
    }
  ],
